#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278380 :question/problem "![](images/05/059cec995f7ef75b56b8c512af09051fe4631733e87d98d25065144500613f73.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $17{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{46}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 63\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{46}^{∘} \\\\  & =22.659… \\\\  & \\approx 23{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =23-17 \\\\  & =6{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278381 :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef8379b21526455a723369fda4360e2d7d3ef3cf54ce7d7767df7bd160a169f4.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $5\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}\\,b}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{5\\sqrt{3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{5\\sqrt{3}}\\,b}{4} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{b}{4} \\\\ b & =4\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=4\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278383 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b95571772af958b4f7b7dcdbba630b78e029c838340dc272809a6c7915dc9c90.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=6\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,b}{2} \\\\ \\frac{21\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}{\\cancel{2}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,b}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ 3b & =21 \\\\ b & =7\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=7\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278384 :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta (6-4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278385 :question/problem "![](images/63/636c9e587d6cbb9fcf9b24d068520330d5365cf80a2800bdd8244698512be3c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $5,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{3/2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{r}]}_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278388 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cd21fbe460f530f08bc8e85f407f9d6ac49b263d3a5361af093a21cd1ca8d11.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is a half annulus enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the half-annulus between the disks of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/da/da18aafec278b5d44b0530097729e4cb4b319e219c005fc155d78fab8aa90937.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{3}^{5}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta (\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{81}{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{[-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =136\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{272}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278391 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u,\\,z=v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278393 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278394 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{8}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278395 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278411 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=6.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(5u+v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278412 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =6xyz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278425 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u-v,\\,z=0\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =xy+z\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=0} \\\\  & =(u+v)(u-v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278427 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=u+v,\\,z=2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =2xz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\cdot 3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278442 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-3v,\\,z=4v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-8,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-8,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2uv\\cdot 10\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278443 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2u+v,\\,y=2u-2v,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $1\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278446 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-uv\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+(-2uv)\\cdot (-4uv)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+8{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278448 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(2+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+(u-v+{v}^{2})\\cdot (-2v)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{u}^{2}v+2u{v}^{2}-2u{v}^{3}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-v+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278449 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨3u{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le u\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩|}_{x=3u{v}^{2}\\,y=u-v\\,z=2} \\\\  & =⟨{(3u{v}^{2})}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =9{u}^{2}{v}^{4}\\cdot 0+(3u{v}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-6uv-3{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(u+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}u]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6(-2+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278450 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-v)+uv\\cdot (-2{u}^{2})+v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-3uv-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4}v+{u}^{2}v]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[-3{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278455 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2v \\\\ 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-2uv} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\\cdot ⟨2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (2u+2v)+0\\cdot (2u+2v)+(v-u)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}-u{v}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}v-\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278459 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 0$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨-(u+v)-(-v),\\hspace{0.222em}u+v-(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}-(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =(-u)\\cdot 1+2u\\cdot 1+v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-u+2u+2v \\\\  & =u+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(u+2v)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u+2v)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[uv+{v}^{2}]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(u+1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+u]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278461 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1-2u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278462 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278463 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278464 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x-{y}^{2}z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278465 :question/problem "![](images/97/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278468 :question/problem "![](images/01/0148ca16ee12a0f651c91091715b4ec07d95639530defb8ed366bacb33e4e87d.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}(1+1) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278469 :question/problem "![](images/52/5276afad13c3e52854d33a3ff3404ab401781e08d1888214121cf2d56c0bb304.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+2y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278470 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{3}+3v⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}+bu+c\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩{|}_{x=u+v\\,y={u}^{2}+2v\\,z={u}^{3}+3v} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2-2u⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (6u-6{u}^{2})+0\\cdot (3{u}^{2}-3)+2\\cdot (2-2u) \\\\  & =6u-6{u}^{2}+4-4u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{2}+2u+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6)\\cdot {u}^{2}+2\\cdot u+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+2+4=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278471 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{e}^{v}+b{e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{-v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{-v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x=u{e}^{v}\\,y=u{e}^{-v}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\cdot ⟨u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩ \\\\  & =(u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{-v}+(u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{v}+u\\cdot (-2u) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{e}^{-v}+{u}^{2}+{e}^{v}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}-{e}^{-v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot {e}^{v}+(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1-1=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278475 :question/problem "![](images/5b/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278477 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d6cedcc2d8c5a232d8d7ed89f050fa7a545ed0da6075ddb44eec67e065b9e87.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=-4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278478 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.167em}xy(y-2x)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.167em}xy(y-2x)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278479 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}xyz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{2}xy(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(-2{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2})\\sqrt{5}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278480 :question/problem "![](images/92/9215246969b621002a96dec3aed8a012045b8061371e4c6a1234f54d53de29cc.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{3}y$ whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278481 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(4y)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278486 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d19d54d0e76ced24d1bd9f865578b72f2b8d67414c8506499c6ae7b1300a6275.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-2x+2y$ and whose domain is the left half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278487 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-2\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(2xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{2}]_{y=-2}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278488 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
